Bakelite Prana

Ingredients: (ROOTS) REGGAE + PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + 432Hz RELAXING MEDITATIVE STYLES + Bassoon + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a corrupted tape cartridge exhumed from a 1955 fallout shelter time capsule.

Bakelite Prana sounds like an Eisenhower-era orchestral broadcast slowed to an oceanic, meditative simmer, then forced into the relentless forward crawl of an underground Berlin club. A seismic 126 BPM progressive house kick drum drives a half-time roots reggae one-drop, anchored not by electric bass, but by the reedy, subterranean groan of an amplified contrabassoon vibrating at 432Hz. Mid-century crooner vocal harmonies float like warm tobacco ghost-frequencies through cavernous spring reverbs, punctuated by off-beat hollow-body guitar chops and the gentle chime of meditation bells.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 124–128 BPM. It utilizes a hypnotic, rolling four-on-the-floor progressive house pulse that secretly carries the sluggish, stoned backbeat and dropped third-beat emphasis of classic Jamaican roots reggae.
- Harmonic Palette: Strictly calibrated to the warm, biological resonance of A=432Hz. Chord structures pull from 1950s sweet jazz and close-harmony vocal arrangements (minor 9ths, major 7ths with flattened 5ths), played on felt-muted upright pianos and layered woodwinds that cycle endlessly in meditative, modal loops.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Oxide Drag: Artificial Wow-and-Flutter generated by physically braking 1/4-inch magnetic tape reels by hand, imparting an intoxicating, sea-sick microtonal wobble to modern progressive house build-ups.
  2. The Resonant Well: Sub-bass frequencies are exclusively generated by acoustic bassoons and contrabassoons close-mic'd through 1950s ribbon transducers and pushed through saturated tube limiters until the woodgrain rattle becomes a warm 30Hz wall of pressure.
  3. Chamber-Dub Spill: Replacing traditional synth white noise sweeps with infinite tape-loop risers made of 1950s test-tone oscillators and distant brass flourishes, fed into oversized subterranean concrete reverb chambers.
- Dynamics: Glacial, twenty-minute breathing arcs. Rather than jarring electronic drops, the music swells organically through additive woodwind layers and tape hiss, exhaling into sudden, cavernous dub craters of extreme low end and tranquil stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement ignited in the late 2020s after an amateur archaeology collective in Truth or Consequences, New Mexico, unearthed a welded steel time capsule buried beneath a 1955 civil defense bunker. Inside rested a severely melted, mold-bloomed magnetic tape labeled "PROJECT TRANQUILITY: CONELRAD SLEEP FREQUENCIES." When played on a restored Ampex deck, the degraded tape fused patriotic easy-listening brass, an anomalous imported mento rhythm, and decaying magnetic flutter into an inadvertent, hypnotic trance. Within months, an underground sect known as the "Spool-Saints" colonized disused subterranean broadcast bunkers across the American Southwest and industrial Manchester. Devotees dress in bespoke 1950s wool gabardine slacks, vintage laboratory smocks, and woven raffia headwraps, dancing barefoot on clay-dusted concrete floors illuminated only by the faint orange filament glow of vacuum tubes and burning frankincense, syncing their breath to the 432Hz low-end purr.

Typical Instruments: Contrabassoon, German-system bassoon (fitted with custom contact pickups), RCA 77-DX ribbon microphones, 1954 Gibson ES-175 hollow-body electric guitar, Tibetan and crystal singing bowls, Roland RE-201 Space Echo loaded with oxidized mid-century tape stock, brushed jazz snare drum, Hammond M3 organ, upright piano with heavy damper felt.

